4. DYNAMIC SCHEMA DESIGN
------------------------------------------------
Columns = fixed core + SORTED union of attr keys across all rows (separately
for nodes and edges).  Sorted (never insertion-ordered) so the schema is
deterministic regardless of dict iteration order.  New attrs added later
(T9: base_score / unit_type / rebuild_order on nodes) become columns with no
change to this code — persist AFTER enrichment.

Final node columns for a full-model graph (box + hole + curved + component):
  node_id (TEXT PK), label (TEXT),
  component_id INTEGER, containing_face_id TEXT, face_count INTEGER,
  face_index INTEGER, plane_id TEXT, area_cm2 DOUBLE, normal_x/y/z DOUBLE,
  centroid TEXT(JSON), vertex_count INTEGER, triangle_count INTEGER,
  interior_angles TEXT(JSON), convexity TEXT, is_base_candidate BOOLEAN,
  is_articulation_point BOOLEAN, mean_curvature DOUBLE, curve_type TEXT,
  is_filled BOOLEAN
Final edge columns:
  source TEXT, target TEXT, relation TEXT,
  dihedral_angle_deg DOUBLE, convexity TEXT, shared_edge_length DOUBLE,
  orientation TEXT, shared_vertex TEXT(JSON), shared_vertex_count INTEGER,
  normal_dot DOUBLE, normal_x/y/z DOUBLE, extrusion_direction TEXT(JSON)
(columns only appear when the union actually contains them — e.g. a box has
no normal_dot column because no box pair carries it.)

Type mapping (per column, from the non-None values):
  bool -> BOOLEAN (checked before int: bool subclasses int)
  int  -> INTEGER, int/float mix -> DOUBLE (ints widened at bind time)
  str  -> TEXT
  list/dict/tuple -> TEXT via json.dumps; round-trip with json.loads
  None (missing attr) -> NULL
All values bound with ? placeholders (executemany); column names validated
by _safe_ident.  No f-string/format of values anywhere.

5. TRANSACTION + LRU SEMANTICS
------------------------------------------------
- duckdb.connect(":memory:"); conn.execute("BEGIN") ... DDL + index + inserts
  ... conn.execute("COMMIT"); on any exception ROLLBACK + conn.close() then
  re-raise (atomic; partial persists never cached).
- On success: close any existing connection for the key (no leak), store new
  conn, move_to_end (MRU), then while len > MAX_GRAPHS: popitem(last=False)
  (oldest) + close.  MAX_GRAPHS = 16.
- _get_graph_db: move_to_end then return the SAME connection object (identity
  — verified by test).  Raises KeyError with:
  "no graph built for mesh '<key>'. Call structure_graph first (graphs are
  ephemeral: the MCP server restart drops them)"
- _get_graph_db never opens a new connection (T8 mutates the same one).

6. READ-ONLY MODE FINDING (IMPORTANT FOR T8)
------------------------------------------------
Inherited-wisdom hint claimed "duckdb supports SET access_mode='read_only'
on an existing in-memory connection".  EMPIRICALLY FALSE for duckdb 1.5.5:

  py -3.14 probe results:
  - conn.execute("SET access_mode='read_only'")            -> InvalidInputException
      "Cannot change access_mode setting while database is running - it must
       be set when opening or attaching the database"
  - SET GLOBAL access_mode -> same error
  - duckdb.connect(":memory:", read_only=True)             -> CatalogException
      "Cannot launch in-memory database in read-only mode!"
  - duckdb_settings() shows access_mode = 'automatic' (GLOBAL scope, fixed)
  - ATTACH ':memory:' AS x (READ_ONLY)                     -> CatalogException

=> There is NO way to make an already-running in-memory duckdb connection
   read-only in 1.5.5.  WORKAROUND for T8 (documented in learnings): the
   connection is process-local and only the MCP server touches it, so
   "read-only mode" is enforced at the query layer: T8's query tool issues
   only SELECT statements (a helper that rejects non-read statements, or
   simply never issuing writes).  The T6 test test_duckdb_read_only_query
   locks the actually-required contract: plain SELECTs work on the cached
   connection and are deterministic (repeatable result sets).

